  "query_preview": "..\n\nGood subsidence and dry mid levels (a 48 C dew pt depression at 700 \nMB per the 00z RAOB) are in place this morning over southeast \nMichigan, with expansive high pressure in control of the Great Lakes \nregion. Transient low clouds seen traversing through southeast \nMichigan, as northerly flow off Lake Huron moistens the low levels. \nSlow return easterly flow around the departing high on Saturday will \nlikely facilitate at least scattered Cu field, helping to hold temps \nin the mid 60s, as 925 MB temps progged to reach 11 C. \n\nA shortwave entering the Northern Plains early this morning is on \ntrack to move through southeast Michigan 12-16z Sunday. Moisture is \nlacking, as the 850 MB moisture gradient/theta-e remains along the \nOhio River. The front will have a narrowing/weakening moisture axis \ncoming out of the Central Plains/Midwest; however, it remains \nsupportive of low chance/scattered pops. Chances will be highest \nover northern half of the CWA, closer to the mid level cold pool (- \n18 C at 500 MB), with several hires solutions indicating light \nshowers in the 8-15z window with the wave of isentropic ascent. The \nactual cold front/trough does not move through until late in the \nday, where local probabilistic guidance also suggests a low chance \nof showers around Saginaw Bay. \n\nModel guidance (Euro/CMC) trends to close out the weekend into early \nnext week are for a more with amplified northern-stream trough to \nswing through the Great Lakes region into New England on Monday, \nwith the parent 500 MB closed low over Quebec. This leads to 850 MB \ntemps temps progged to reach 0 to 2 C Monday morning, setting us up \nfor a cold/frost night Monday night with center of high pressure \narriving over southeast Michigan around midnight. Warm advection \nhigh clouds with the strong warm front over the western Great Lakes \nlooks to be the only potential hindrance to temps falling into the \nmid 30s.",
